@asmeurer I think the point is that a single object could contain the same Dummy more than once (think of a variable of integration or my silly evaluate=False example).
Pre-serialization this is fine.
But upon S(srepr(x)), they become two distinct objects.  Bad news for the integral, whose meaning has just completely changed.

Having spent considerable time this evening in the cold embrace of TeX SE threads, I think the best option may be:

Use \operatorname when appropriate. This seems to be much less problematic than the other options, although it only makes sense for operators. I think this would have been the right choice for #22088. The extraneous space after ‘Range’ is caused by \left (see #23286), not \operatorname, and to my eyes the extra space before ‘Range’ is advantageous. For example – although 3 Range is nonsense –

\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\begin{document}
\[3\operatorname{Range}(3, 2)\]
\[3\text{Range}(3, 2)\]
\[3\operatorname{Range}\left(3, 2\right)\]
\[3\text{Range}\left(3, 2\right)\]
\end{document}
produces:


Use \text for embedded text, for some definition of that word. This probably includes the words in Piecewise.
Use \mathrm for everything else. This isn’t nice in sans-serif environments, but \text is approximately equally bad in italic environments. I’m not sure which of these is most common, but post-processing \mathrm to \mathsf is easier than trying to do that for half of the \texts.
